# Communist Nerves — Two Comrades, One Mesh

```elixir
# SETUP — this notebook's runtime is comrade A.
# Pulls the project in as a dependency but starts nothing
# (start_applications: false). Each comrade is configured and started
# explicitly below. The install config never changes between runs, so after
# the first run this cell is served from cache: no network, no recompile.

project = "/Users/sahilpohare/p2p/p2p_clustering/elixir_rpc"

Mix.install([{:elixir_rpc, path: project, env: :dev}], start_applications: false)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
:logger.set_primary_config(:level, :warning)

# Partisan needs a free TCP port per comrade; ask the OS for one.
free_port = fn ->
  {:ok, socket} = :gen_tcp.listen(0, reuseaddr: true)
  {:ok, port} = :inet.port(socket)
  :gen_tcp.close(socket)
  port
end

alias ElixirRpc.{CodeIdentity, DemoWorker, Network}

# One fleet ID shared by both comrades: signed records from any endpoint in
# this fleet are enrolled automatically, so nobody configures the other.
fleet = Base.encode16(:crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32), case: :lower)
root = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "comrades-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")

"comrade A is #{node()}"
```

## 1. Boot comrade B

A second, completely separate BEAM on this machine. It gets our code path, so
it runs byte-identical modules — which is exactly what the code hash will prove
later. B is born knowing nothing about the mesh.

```elixir
# Start a sibling BEAM with the same code path and cookie.
# :peer is only the local launcher; B still has to find A on its own over Iroh.
[_, host] = node() |> Atom.to_string() |> String.split("@")

{:ok, b_pid, b} =
  :peer.start(%{
    name: :"comrade_b_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
    host: String.to_charlist(host),
    longnames: true,
    args:
      [~c"-setcookie", Atom.to_charlist(Node.get_cookie())] ++
        Enum.flat_map(:code.get_path(), &[~c"-pa", &1])
  })

# Keep B's console quiet too.
:erpc.call(b, :logger, :set_primary_config, [:level, :warning])

"comrade B is #{b}"
```

## 2. Both comrades join the mesh

Each comrade starts the same three things:

* an **Iroh daemon** — its signed identity, plus mDNS, DHT, DNS and relay discovery
* **Partisan** — the BEAM overlay that Horde places work over
* a **TalkWorker** — republishes its signed capability record every 3 seconds

A starts as a camera. B starts with no capabilities at all.

```elixir
# The whole app configuration for one comrade, as plain data.
# Passed to Application.put_all_env/1 — on A locally, on B via :erpc.
comrade_env = fn name, partisan_port, capabilities ->
  name = Atom.to_string(name)

  [
    partisan: [
      name: String.to_atom(name),
      listen_addrs: [%{ip: {127, 0, 0, 1}, port: partisan_port}],
      peer_service_manager: :partisan_hyparview_peer_service_manager,
      hyparview: [active_min_size: 3, active_max_size: 6, passive_max_size: 30],
      channels: [:membership, :rpc, :discovery],
      tls: false
    ],
    elixir_rpc: [
      # :iroh starts the discovery daemon + Horde; talk_worker publishes our record.
      network_mode: :iroh,
      iroh_discovery: [
        data_dir: Path.join(root, name),
        fleet_id: fleet,
        node_name: name
      ],
      talk_worker: [capabilities: capabilities],
      # Modules this comrade offers to run for others; their md5s go in the record.
      placeable_modules: [DemoWorker],
      # First spawn joins Partisan and waits for Horde membership to sync.
      handoff_timeout: 15_000,
      # Provisioned trust: advertised names we may place work on. Discovery
      # never mints atoms from what strangers claim.
      authorized_nodes: %{Atom.to_string(node()) => node(), Atom.to_string(b) => b}
    ]
  ]
end

# Comrade A: configure, then boot the app for the first time.
:ok = Application.put_all_env(comrade_env.(node(), free_port.(), %{"camera" => true}))
{:ok, _} = Application.ensure_all_started(:elixir_rpc)

# Comrade B: same config shape, remote calls only — no closures cross the wire.
:ok = :erpc.call(b, Application, :put_all_env, [comrade_env.(b, free_port.(), %{})])
{:ok, _} = :erpc.call(b, Application, :ensure_all_started, [:elixir_rpc])

# Partisan resets the log level when it boots; quiet both comrades again.
:logger.set_primary_config(:level, :warning)
:erpc.call(b, :logger, :set_primary_config, [:level, :warning])

"both comrades are on the mesh"
```

## 3. Auto-discovery — A finds B

Nobody told A where B is. Iroh finds the other daemon (mDNS on the LAN), the
two gossip their signed records, and each side verifies the other's signature
before trusting anything in it. Re-run this cell until B shows up (~2 s).

```elixir
# Everyone this comrade has discovered and verified, minus itself.
# B has no capabilities yet — only its code hashes.
{:ok, %{"peers" => peers}} = Network.capabilities()

for peer <- peers, peer["node_name"] != Atom.to_string(node()) do
  %{node: peer["node_name"], capabilities: peer["capabilities"], sequence: peer["sequence"]}
end
```

## 4. Advertise — B declares a GPU

B publishes a new signed snapshot. The code hashes ride along automatically.

```elixir
# Runs on B: replace B's capability snapshot and publish it immediately.
:erpc.call(b, Network, :advertise, [[gpu: true, vram_mb: 8_192]])
```

```elixir
# Back on A: the new record has arrived, with a higher signed sequence number.
{:ok, %{"peers" => peers}} = Network.capabilities()
for peer <- peers, peer["node_name"] == Atom.to_string(b), do: peer
```

## 5. Get work done — A spawns, B computes

One line. A asks for "any GPU comrade holding my exact `DemoWorker`". The
Ministry of Routing picks B, Partisan joins the two, Horde places the task on
B, and the result comes home.

```elixir
# {module, function, args} — never a closure. DemoWorker.report/2 sends
# {id, {:demo_result, id, node()}} back to us, so node() says where it ran.
{:ok, _pid} = Network.spawn([gpu: true], {DemoWorker, :report, [self(), :frame_42]})

receive do
  {:frame_42, {:demo_result, :frame_42, ran_on}} -> "ran on #{ran_on}"
after
  10_000 -> "no answer — re-run section 3 and check B is listed"
end
```

## 6. Trust — the Ministry checks your papers

B only offered `DemoWorker`. Ask it to run anything else and there is no
match in the signed record, so nothing is ever sent.

```elixir
# Kernel is not in B's placeable_modules, so no peer advertises Kernel's hash.
Network.spawn([gpu: true], {Kernel, :node, []})
```

```elixir
# What placement demanded vs what B actually signed.
%{
  demanded: CodeIdentity.requirement(Kernel),
  b_offers: :erpc.call(b, CodeIdentity, :advertised, [])
}
```

## 7. Tear down

```elixir
# Stop comrade B's BEAM and our networking; delete both daemons' keys.
:peer.stop(b_pid)
Application.stop(:elixir_rpc)
File.rm_rf!(root)
:ok
```
